Z||000002||Jay||04-15-2001||09:16 PM||||Do it!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!But you'll have to convert it to a 95 or earlier to bypass OBDII (throttle body, tps, O2's)<BR>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT <A HREF="http://www.members.home.net/jaygrande" TARGET=_blank>www.members.home.net/jaygrande</A> <BR>||||reg||14
Z||000003||Will||04-15-2001||09:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by blade56_2001:<BR><B>  What would ya'll say If I could get my hands on a '98 v8 northstar with less than 5000 yes thats right 5000 miles on it...... is this a good buy!  <BR>The only problem is the motor was in a flood..<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If you can start it on the stand, go for it. Just remember to take the plugs out and turn it over a few times before you try to start it. I think you should start it to get oil back into the engine parts. There is probably no water in the oiling system, but you won't know until you do something about it.<P>Also, put a squirt or two of ATF in each cylinder and turn it over a few times before trying to start it.<P>How much is it, BTW?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mach10||04-15-2001||11:53 PM||||Uh, I'd be worried if that engine has sat in any water for more than 12 hours. I heard that if it sits longer than that, then you get rust-spots on various steel and iron parts, and those quickly turn into hotspots which kill the engine. If there is water in the pan I have it from a good source that you will need to have everything machined to ensure that the motor doesn't tear itself apart later. Oh, and any electronic parts will most likely be shot...||||reg||4
